window.docContents[71].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties":{"contents":"* Oscillators A and B have the same sort, harmonic oscillator (theorem) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#oscillator_context_and_sort.* The designations of A and B are different, and a designation that gives every oscillator the same name is not injective, so it cannot be a Designated instance (theorem) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#designation.* For each oscillator o, omegaSqOf divides o's spring constant by o's mass with IndividualQuantity.div and type-checks as an IndividualQuantity of kind omegaSquaredK about o, whose magnitude is o's k/m (type-checked) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#oscillator_indexed_quantities.* Oscillator B's mass, supplied where a mass about A is expected, fails with a type mismatch: as a value, as an argument of IndividualQuantity.add or IndividualQuantity.div, and when oscillator A of rig R₁ is mixed with oscillator A of rig R₂ (refused) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#refused_cross_oscillator.* Subtracting an earlier record of A's mass from a later one, both on rig R₁, type-checks as an IndividualQuantity of kind massK about A, using DifferenceKind.ofScale; its magnitude is the later mass minus the earlier mass (type-checked) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#same_rig_estimate_difference.* Dedicating a kind to an object whose type has no Sorted instance fails to type-check (refused) HCP.L1_TwoBodies#refused_unsorted_object.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.2.1.3. Verifiable properties","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties"},"/The-Proved-Spine/#proved-spine":{"contents":"This chapter records what PropertyKindCalculus already establishes, sorry-free, in its\nMathlib-free core. Every node here links to a real declaration with\n(lean := \"PropertyKindCalculus.…\"), so its proved status in the dependency graph is\nread directly from the checked library rather than asserted in prose.\n\nThe point to keep in view: each of these is a law or an operation, not a class\nmembership. A description-logic reasoner (OWL2/SROIQ) can record that width\nspecializes length; it cannot state, let alone prove, that specialization is\ntransitive or that operator availability is monotone in scale. Those are the\nentries below. The systems those properties characterize — and the part–whole and\nfour-category structure they carry — are the foundations chapter's subject.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"6. The Proved Spine","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/#proved-spine"}});